          "textLabel": "The 2001 tax law enhances the benefits of saving with a <GLOSSREF key=\"CoverdellESA\">Coverdell ESA</GLOSSREF> or <GLOSSREF key=\"QTP\">qualified tuition plans</GLOSSREF>. You can save for college in a qualified tuition plan.<br><br>It appears as if you will not be able to contribute to a Coverdell ESA. This is because your indicated <GLOSSREF key=\"MAGI\">MAGI</GLOSSREF> is above the income limitations for your tax filing status.<br><br>For persons filing a single return, allowable contributions to a Coverdell ESA begin to phase out when modified adjustable gross income (MAGI) reaches $95,000. Allowable contributions are completely phased out if MAGI is $110,000 or more.<br><br>Beginning in 2002, the income phase-out limits for married persons filing a joint return increase to $190,000 and $220,000, respectively.<br><br>You can also save for the college education of a child or grandchild with an <GLOSSREF key=\"UGMAUTMA\">UGMA/UTMA</GLOSSREF> account.<br><br>You may also wish to save for college with a <GLOSSREF key=\"taxableAccount\">taxable account</GLOSSREF> that invests in stocks, bonds, and mutual funds."
